267
00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:54.700
You can use ramen noodles as your crunchies.

268
00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:55.260
Wow.

269
00:09:55.580 --> 00:09:56.120
There you go.

270
00:09:56.280 --> 00:09:59.620
Some more information from Lee about this being his first episode.

271
00:09:59.620 --> 00:10:08.080
He said they were terrified because remember we talked about how they break down the episodes as a group and then writers get assigned to write the episode?

272
00:10:08.340 --> 00:10:17.040
He said that since their episode was episode six, they had very little in their outline.

273
00:10:17.460 --> 00:10:24.700
He said they had fleshed out the other episodes much more than this one, and he thought that they were being set up to fail.

274
00:10:24.920 --> 00:10:26.060
They were being tested.

275
00:10:26.060 --> 00:10:33.240
Yes, exactly, because he was like, how do you give the staff writers with the least amount of experience the least worked on outline?

276
00:10:33.420 --> 00:10:34.220
Yes, exactly.

277
00:10:34.420 --> 00:10:43.360
He said after they turned in their script and they had to get their notes on their script, Greg had pneumonia, and they had to go to Greg's house in order to get their notes.

278
00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:52.280
And he said the thing that really stood out to him was that he explained the idea of the Jim-Pam subtext to Gene and Lee.

279
00:10:52.460 --> 00:11:00.780
They had written a really on-the-nose Jim-Pam scene, and Greg said, sometimes just a look is enough.

280
00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:07.500
And he said it kind of blew their minds and that Greg completely brought that idea to them.

281
00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:15.200
And in the episode, something I remember is when Pam is reading Jim's palm, that was a big deal.

282
00:11:15.460 --> 00:11:24.000
It was how – there was always this discussion, how can Jim and Pam physically touch without it being inappropriate?

283
00:11:24.460 --> 00:11:24.640
Right.

284
00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:27.780
Well, lady, I think that was very intimate.

285
00:11:28.020 --> 00:11:32.460
I even looked up online about hand-touching, and it is considered physical intimacy.

286
00:11:32.580 --> 00:11:34.280
That was some major flirting.

287
00:11:34.400 --> 00:11:35.940
That's a note card I have for later.

288
00:11:35.940 --> 00:11:36.400
All right.

289
00:11:36.840 --> 00:11:38.600
Should we move on to fast fact number two?

290
00:11:38.720 --> 00:11:39.320
Let's do it.

291
00:11:39.480 --> 00:11:39.700
All right.

292
00:11:39.840 --> 00:11:44.460
Fast fact number two, Sensei Ira was played by Lance Kroll.

293
00:11:44.800 --> 00:11:46.880
Is it Sensei or Sensei?

294
00:11:49.660 --> 00:11:50.860
Oh, my gosh.

295
00:11:51.180 --> 00:11:54.520
It's Sensei Ira was played by Lance Kroll, who's a friend of mine.

296
00:11:54.520 --> 00:11:58.980
And I have five note cards on him because he and I talked on the phone yesterday.

297
00:11:59.220 --> 00:12:03.940
Oh, you know that I was very starstruck by him when he was on our episode.

298
00:12:03.940 --> 00:12:04.640
I know.

299
00:12:04.660 --> 00:12:08.040
He even said, I think Jenna watched a lot of Joe Schmo.

300
00:12:08.300 --> 00:12:08.720
Yes.

301
00:12:08.940 --> 00:12:14.080
That cracks me up that he said that because I did corner him at lunch one day, and I made

302
00:12:14.080 --> 00:12:16.120
him tell me everything about the Joe Schmo show.

303
00:12:16.360 --> 00:12:18.920
If you guys have not seen this, please go look it up.

304
00:12:19.420 --> 00:12:28.160
The Joe Schmo show was a fake reality show contest where everybody on the show were actors

305
00:12:28.160 --> 00:12:29.940
except for one person.

306
00:12:30.140 --> 00:12:31.480
The Joe Schmo guy.

307
00:12:31.660 --> 00:12:31.940
Yes.

308
00:12:32.100 --> 00:12:33.300
Was a real person.

309
00:12:33.520 --> 00:12:37.040
And everyone around him was acting like they were in a reality show.

310
00:12:37.220 --> 00:12:37.480
Yes.

311
00:12:37.600 --> 00:12:42.760
And they were locked in this house, and they had to do competitions and vote each other

312
00:12:42.760 --> 00:12:43.700
off at the end.

313
00:12:44.380 --> 00:12:47.560
By the way, Kristen Wiig is on that show with Lance.

314
00:12:47.800 --> 00:12:51.480
A lot of great comedians and improvisers that I knew were on that show.

315
00:12:51.600 --> 00:12:52.760
I auditioned for that.

316
00:12:52.760 --> 00:12:59.140
It was cast by Alison Jones, and I was devastated when I did not get it because I thought, what

317
00:12:59.140 --> 00:13:05.380
an amazing acting experience to live in a house and have to play this character 24-7.

318
00:13:05.640 --> 00:13:07.460
And by the way, the show is brilliant.

319
00:13:07.720 --> 00:13:09.800
So yes, I am starstruck by Lance.

320
00:13:09.920 --> 00:13:10.980
I can't believe you know him.

321
00:13:11.140 --> 00:13:12.640
I can't believe I didn't know that you know him.

322
00:13:12.780 --> 00:13:16.320
So I emailed him yesterday, and I was like, Lance, we're going to be talking about the

323
00:13:16.320 --> 00:13:16.660
fight.

324
00:13:17.100 --> 00:13:18.000
Where are you?

325
00:13:18.260 --> 00:13:19.400
Can you hop on the phone?

326
00:13:19.520 --> 00:13:21.340
And he was like, I'm actually in Atlanta.

327
00:13:21.340 --> 00:13:26.380
He has moved to Atlanta, and he set up his own production company because he's really

328
00:13:26.380 --> 00:13:26.740
talented.

329
00:13:26.920 --> 00:13:32.560
He's an actor, comedian, writer, and he has a production company in Atlanta called Picture

330
00:13:32.560 --> 00:13:33.320
It Productions.

331
00:13:34.160 --> 00:13:34.880
How about that?

332
00:13:34.940 --> 00:13:37.060
You can check it out at pictureitproductions.com.

333
00:13:37.380 --> 00:13:38.100
All right, Lance.

334
00:13:38.500 --> 00:13:43.540
So we met so long ago, and I said, Lance, did we meet doing improv?

335
00:13:43.540 --> 00:13:45.460
I can't remember exactly how we met.

336
00:13:45.600 --> 00:13:49.340
And he remembered exactly, and it made me laugh so hard.

337
00:13:49.340 --> 00:13:49.720
What?

338
00:13:50.100 --> 00:13:51.380
Okay, so he was friends.

339
00:13:51.520 --> 00:13:56.460
We had mutual friends, and I went into an editing bay where a friend of ours worked,

340
00:13:56.540 --> 00:13:58.600
and they were helping me with my acting reel.

341
00:13:59.020 --> 00:13:59.400
Okay.

342
00:13:59.640 --> 00:14:01.420
And I said, Lance, was this before the office?

343
00:14:01.580 --> 00:14:02.660
He said, no, Angela.

344
00:14:02.920 --> 00:14:06.420
It was like after the pilot, but you really didn't think the show was going to go.

345
00:14:07.840 --> 00:14:13.480
So you were trying to put some of your office scene with Kevin and accounting in your reel?

346
00:14:13.700 --> 00:14:15.060
Should we say what a reel is?

347
00:14:15.240 --> 00:14:15.900
Yes, yes.

348
00:14:15.900 --> 00:14:20.540
So just for people who don't know, like, you know, actors have a headshot, and on the back

349
00:14:20.540 --> 00:14:27.400
is their resume, and you give that at auditions, but you also would send a link to clips of

350
00:14:27.400 --> 00:14:30.320
various performances you had been on, and that's your acting reel.

351
00:14:30.460 --> 00:14:31.300
It helps you get jobs.

352
00:14:31.500 --> 00:14:36.480
So, Angela, you were putting together your acting reel with some clips from the office.

353
00:14:36.520 --> 00:14:36.720
With some clips.

354
00:14:36.880 --> 00:14:41.260
And this really dates me and our show because it was all on VHS.

355
00:14:41.480 --> 00:14:42.340
Oh, my gosh.

356
00:14:42.340 --> 00:14:44.500
There was a guy actually editing it.

357
00:14:44.500 --> 00:14:48.660
Yes, and I had brought in, like, a box of all of my footage, and they were putting together

358
00:14:48.660 --> 00:14:50.180
a montage of my scenes.

359
00:14:50.480 --> 00:14:54.960
And Lance said, Angela, it was so cute and charming because you turned to me and you said,

360
00:14:55.020 --> 00:14:56.040
hey, will you watch this?

361
00:14:56.280 --> 00:14:57.660
What do you think of my reel?

362
00:14:57.900 --> 00:15:03.380
And he said, Ange, it was every second you've ever been on screen.

363
00:15:03.920 --> 00:15:05.860
He said it was so long.

364
00:15:06.140 --> 00:15:08.780
And also, in some of it, you didn't even have dialogue.

365
00:15:08.780 --> 00:15:10.140
You just walked by.

366
00:15:10.820 --> 00:15:11.880
That is hilarious.

367
00:15:12.020 --> 00:15:15.360
There was, like, a commercial, like I did for Chrysler, where I walked by and I just

368
00:15:15.360 --> 00:15:18.180
went, and I, like, did thumbs up at someone.

369
00:15:18.840 --> 00:15:21.480
I was like, oh, my gosh, Lance, it's so embarrassing.

370
00:15:21.480 --> 00:15:22.960
But that's the first time we met.

371
00:15:23.200 --> 00:15:27.880
So, it was after the pilot when I thought the office wasn't going to go, and I was trying

372
00:15:27.880 --> 00:15:29.240
to figure out how to get more work.

373
00:15:29.420 --> 00:15:32.480
That's very funny that then he ends up being on the show.

374
00:15:32.660 --> 00:15:33.060
I know.

375
00:15:33.200 --> 00:15:36.920
And then he told me, he said, Ange, did you know I tested for the role of Jim?

376
00:15:38.000 --> 00:15:39.640
He tested for Jim.

377
00:15:39.640 --> 00:15:40.080
What?

378
00:15:40.380 --> 00:15:42.780
So, I must have done scenes with him.

379
00:15:42.960 --> 00:15:45.760
He said you weren't in his group on his test day.

380
00:15:45.920 --> 00:15:46.120
Oh, okay.

381
00:15:46.140 --> 00:15:50.820
He had a different group, and Bob Odenkirk was in his group for Michael Scott.

382
00:15:51.000 --> 00:15:51.740
Oh, yes.

383
00:15:51.760 --> 00:15:52.440
That's right.

384
00:15:52.440 --> 00:15:54.620
I was in the Steve Carell test group.

385
00:15:54.780 --> 00:15:57.760
Well, you know something that's funny about Bob Odenkirk, since you brought him up?

386
00:15:58.040 --> 00:16:02.560
I knew Bob Odenkirk when I was auditioning for the office, and we were both called.

387
00:16:02.680 --> 00:16:05.720
I was called to do Pam, and he was called to do Michael.

388
00:16:05.720 --> 00:16:08.800
Michael, so we got together, and we practiced together.

389
00:16:09.080 --> 00:16:09.460
Aw.

390
00:16:09.760 --> 00:16:15.760
But then I wasn't in his test group, and I had thought, darn it, we worked a bunch of

391
00:16:15.760 --> 00:16:16.460
bits together.

392
00:16:16.460 --> 00:16:18.140
We were so excited to show them off.

393
00:16:18.140 --> 00:16:23.800
But he did this very funny audition where he brought in his guitar, and he sang a song

394
00:16:23.800 --> 00:16:27.400
to Pam, and we had worked it all out, and we were going to ask if we could do it if we

395
00:16:27.400 --> 00:16:29.120
were paired together, but we never were.

396
00:16:29.120 --> 00:16:32.580
So Lance told me this great story that the day that he was screen testing, you know, he's

397
00:16:32.580 --> 00:16:33.380
in the lobby waiting.

398
00:16:33.680 --> 00:16:35.960
They were all kind of giddy that Bob Odenkirk was in their group.

399
00:16:36.080 --> 00:16:39.420
They were all really big fans of Bob's, and Bob walked over to Lance and was like, hey,

400
00:16:39.940 --> 00:16:41.300
you want to run lines with me?

401
00:16:41.800 --> 00:16:43.820
And Lance was like, uh, sure.

402
00:16:44.460 --> 00:16:47.540
And he said it was really cool, and they, like, sort of went out in this lobby and ran

403
00:16:47.540 --> 00:16:50.260
lines, like, three or four times, like, the scenes all the way through.

404
00:16:50.480 --> 00:16:52.020
He just thought it was a really cool moment.

405
00:16:52.160 --> 00:16:56.020
So Lance said when he read for Jim, he was like, you know, I really felt like I just tried

406
00:16:56.020 --> 00:17:00.460
too hard, and he said then when he got called back, when we were, once the show was up and

407
00:17:00.460 --> 00:17:06.100
going for the fight, he read that it was like a martial arts guy, and he goes, oh, my gosh.

408
00:17:06.300 --> 00:17:08.560
Lance's background is actually martial arts.

409
00:17:08.560 --> 00:17:11.220
He was in the martial arts community for 15 years.

410
00:17:11.420 --> 00:17:14.040
He has a third-degree black belt in Taekwondo.

411
00:17:14.400 --> 00:17:18.980
He was the sixth in the nation in his, like, weight category in 1992.

412
00:17:19.680 --> 00:17:22.260
He trained junior Olympic gold medalists.

413
00:17:22.400 --> 00:17:23.040
Like, he was...

414
00:17:23.040 --> 00:17:24.580
Did they know this about him?

415
00:17:24.640 --> 00:17:25.340
I mean, I...

416
00:17:25.340 --> 00:17:26.660
I think they didn't.

417
00:17:26.800 --> 00:17:30.160
I don't know, but he said when he saw who the character was, he was like, oh, my gosh.

418
00:17:30.300 --> 00:17:33.600
He said, I am not going to try to go for the joke at all.

419
00:17:33.600 --> 00:17:37.700
I'm just going to go in as if I'm, like, working with one of my students and teaching

420
00:17:37.700 --> 00:17:38.300
a class.

421
00:17:38.460 --> 00:17:39.400
And that's what he did.

422
00:17:39.400 --> 00:17:41.460
He put no pizzazz on it.

423
00:17:41.580 --> 00:17:44.100
He went in and played it really straight, and they loved it.

424
00:17:44.180 --> 00:17:45.200
They always loved that.

425
00:17:45.420 --> 00:17:48.420
Those are the people they always cast were the people who played it real.

426
00:17:48.660 --> 00:17:51.080
This leads me to my third fast fact, Angela.

427
00:17:51.260 --> 00:17:52.600
I know you might have more to say about Lance.

428
00:17:52.600 --> 00:17:53.060
No, no, no.

429
00:17:53.080 --> 00:17:53.440
Go, go.

430
00:17:53.600 --> 00:17:54.920
But this is a fan question.

431
00:17:54.920 --> 00:17:58.660
Is anyone on the cast trained in martial arts?

432
00:17:58.840 --> 00:17:59.680
That's from Will.

433
00:18:00.960 --> 00:18:01.660
So, yes.

434
00:18:01.800 --> 00:18:08.660
Lance and also Rainn Wilson studied karate his sophomore year in college and is a yellow belt.

435
00:18:08.900 --> 00:18:10.040
I didn't know that.

436
00:18:10.040 --> 00:18:13.980
Yes, and I asked Rainn if the writers knew that, and he said no.

437
00:18:14.780 --> 00:18:19.480
Isn't that what a weird coincidence that they wrote this whole karate-themed episode, and

438
00:18:19.480 --> 00:18:21.800
Rainn is a yellow belt, and Lance was a black belt.

439
00:18:22.180 --> 00:18:22.620
Yes.

440
00:18:22.800 --> 00:18:23.820
That is so wild.

441
00:18:24.060 --> 00:18:24.200
Okay.

442
00:18:24.460 --> 00:18:25.480
Do you have more Lance stuff?

443
00:18:25.480 --> 00:18:28.400
Because I interrupted your Lance cards with my fast fact.

444
00:18:28.880 --> 00:18:31.660
He actually had a really funny thing that made me laugh.

445
00:18:31.760 --> 00:18:36.860
He said that when they got to the actual fight and the choreography of the fight, he

446
00:18:36.860 --> 00:18:41.720
said he was so surprised because there was no choreography.

447
00:18:42.060 --> 00:18:43.460
There was no stunt guy.

448
00:18:43.700 --> 00:18:47.340
He said literally it was like, okay, guys, just two idiots try to hit each other.

449
00:18:47.620 --> 00:18:49.220
This was very early on.

450
00:18:49.320 --> 00:18:50.820
We did not have a budget.

451
00:18:50.820 --> 00:18:51.860
That's what I told him.

452
00:18:52.000 --> 00:18:52.580
For these kinds of things.

453
00:18:52.600 --> 00:18:52.840
I told him.

454
00:18:52.940 --> 00:18:57.420
I said, Lance, this does not surprise me because they put Phil Shea, our prop guy, in a box

455
00:18:57.420 --> 00:18:58.120
for hours.

456
00:18:58.320 --> 00:18:58.480
Yeah.

457
00:18:58.760 --> 00:19:00.440
There was no, like, stunt person.

458
00:19:01.680 --> 00:19:06.200
In the early years of The Office, I always felt like it was a show we were making in

459
00:19:06.200 --> 00:19:07.720
our garage for our neighborhood.

460
00:19:08.120 --> 00:19:11.400
I always felt like it was a one-act play we were doing, like, in someone's garage.

461
00:19:11.620 --> 00:19:12.780
As a kid, I used to do that.

462
00:19:12.840 --> 00:19:16.120
I used to put on plays in my garage, and the garage door was the curtain.

463
00:19:16.320 --> 00:19:16.560
Yeah.

464
00:19:16.560 --> 00:19:21.040
And so, and I would, like, put chairs out on my driveway for the neighborhood kids to

465
00:19:21.040 --> 00:19:21.940
come see my show.

466
00:19:22.160 --> 00:19:24.820
It was like a puppet show or a variety show.

467
00:19:25.000 --> 00:19:28.880
That's what The Office always felt like to me, just like a bunch of us making a show

468
00:19:28.880 --> 00:19:29.640
in our garage.

469
00:19:29.860 --> 00:19:30.040
Yeah.

470
00:19:30.220 --> 00:19:32.340
So this won't be the last time you see Sensei Ira.

471
00:19:32.400 --> 00:19:36.120
He does come back in season nine in the episode Junior Salesman.

472
00:19:36.560 --> 00:19:37.320
He does?

473
00:19:37.600 --> 00:19:38.000
Yes.

474
00:19:38.200 --> 00:19:39.200
What does he do in that episode?

475
00:19:39.440 --> 00:19:41.800
He was actually there to interview for a job.

476
00:19:41.800 --> 00:19:42.880
Oh, I remember that.

477
00:19:42.880 --> 00:19:48.840
Oh, Dwight brings in a bunch of his old friends and weirdos to interview.

478
00:19:49.200 --> 00:19:49.960
And, like, family members.

479
00:19:50.180 --> 00:19:50.440
Yes.

480
00:19:50.740 --> 00:19:55.500
Lance emailed me a great behind-the-scenes photo of him and the other people interviewing

481
00:19:55.500 --> 00:20:03.400
for jobs in the conference room, and he has his karate outfit on over a business suit.

482
00:20:03.540 --> 00:20:04.980
So his tie is, like, poking out.

483
00:20:05.120 --> 00:20:06.160
It's, like, so odd.

484
00:20:06.580 --> 00:20:09.500
But, yeah, he was on season one and then on season nine.

485
00:20:09.500 --> 00:20:13.440
So he sort of had this great perspective of being on the bookends of the show.

486
00:20:13.540 --> 00:20:15.340
What a very, very cool thing.

487
00:20:15.560 --> 00:20:15.740
Yeah.

488
00:20:15.980 --> 00:20:20.560
Thank you so much, Lance, for taking time out of your schedule in Atlanta to chat me up.

489
00:20:20.600 --> 00:20:23.060
And we're going to call you again come Junior Salesman.

490
00:20:23.280 --> 00:20:23.520
Yeah.

491
00:20:23.760 --> 00:20:24.280
I love it.

492
00:20:24.520 --> 00:20:24.820
All right.

493
00:20:24.880 --> 00:20:27.740
Let's take a break, and we'll be back to break down the fight.

494
00:20:28.040 --> 00:20:37.020
We are back.

495
00:20:37.260 --> 00:20:37.560
Hello.

496
00:20:38.220 --> 00:20:40.820
This episode begins with a prank.

497
00:20:41.800 --> 00:20:44.540
Jim pranks Dwight by putting his desk in the bathroom.

498
00:20:45.220 --> 00:20:46.260
Oh, poor Dwight.

499
00:20:46.260 --> 00:20:47.160
This was a good one.

500
00:20:47.160 --> 00:20:48.300
This was a good prank.

501
00:20:48.520 --> 00:20:49.360
I like this one.

502
00:20:49.520 --> 00:20:53.140
You have to look for Kevin in the background at the end when he leaves one of the stalls.

503
00:20:53.460 --> 00:20:55.440
He has a lit candle and a newspaper.

504
00:20:55.800 --> 00:21:01.200
And for all of you office geeks, this was the moment that led to Kevin's line in Office

505
00:21:01.200 --> 00:21:04.580
Olympics about the bathroom candle smelling like cookies.

506
00:21:05.520 --> 00:21:11.260
Also, remember that in the Dundies, he did win the Don't Go In There After Me award.

507
00:21:11.500 --> 00:21:11.840
Yes.

508
00:21:12.160 --> 00:21:13.360
And I think we see why.

509
00:21:13.360 --> 00:21:18.320
